        "etag" : "\"Q44G99shyMl14lSVN9oTpl4EamI/XmAzZiP5mEWBMleRxhjendfFsl0\"",
        "id" : "z13sirhowweuhzzub22rhxrr1yu5xn3rw04.1365647099051283",
        "kind" : "plus#comment",
        "object" : {
          "content" : "<span class=\"proflinkWrapper\"><span class=\"proflinkPrefix\">+</span><a class=\"proflink\" href=\"https://plus.google.com/107457342204653676533\" oid=\"107457342204653676533\">Dillon Nichols</a></span> Thanks. I haven&#39;t done any experimenting yet with a shift register but I&#39;ll have to play around with that chip. I think you&#39;re right that I&#39;ll need something specialized to manage the multiplexing. Only one digit is actually lit at a time (they use persistence of vision to give the appearance of a fully lit display) so I need something to continuously scan through the 9 digits. These are definitely LED. They&#39;re the type used in the original TI-30 calculator.",
